course: dictionary
lesson: 10988
title: Warring: fighting and arguing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Warring» comes from the verb «war». It describes groups who are fighting each other, like two sides who will not stop. In history, «warring» usually means groups fighting a real war. The example sentence is about ancient China, where many rival states fought each other until one state, Qin, united the country.
explain: You can also use «warring» for people who keep arguing. «Warring brothers» are not at war with guns. They quarrel loudly and will not agree. Context helps you choose the right meaning: look at the words around it. If the text mentions armies or kingdoms, it means fighting. If it mentions shouting or arguing, it means quarrelling.
explain: Try the check from before: swap your meaning into the sentence. «In the Warring States period, China was split into rival states fighting each other» still makes sense with fighting, so the meaning fits. «Our warring neighbours shout every morning» makes sense with arguing.
fact: Warring means fighting a war against each other, or arguing strongly.
fact: The verb «war» has these forms: war, wars, warred, warring.
fact: «Warring» can describe kingdoms, tribes, factions, sides, neighbours or brothers.
fact: «In the Spring and Autumn and Warring States periods, 770–221 BC, China was split into rival states fighting each other, until the state of Qin united it in 221 BC.»
fact: Warring sides are groups on opposite sides of a conflict, and warring factions are groups that fight each other within one place or group.
exercise: «In the Warring States periods, China was split into rival states fighting each other.» What does warring mean here? | Sleeping peacefully side by side | *Fighting a war against each other | Trading goods with each other | Building roads together
exercise: Mia says her warring brothers argue every day about the remote. What does warring suggest? | Going on holiday | Cleaning the house together | Playing games together happily | *Quarrelling strongly, like two sides who will not agree
exercise: Which word is another form of the verb «war»? | wagon | wander | warm | *wars
test: In a story, «warring factions» fight for the throne. What are warring factions? | Birds flying south for winter | Friends sharing a picnic | Teachers in a school | *Groups fighting against each other
test: Which sentence uses «warring» for people who keep arguing, not for a real war? | The warring tribes lit their fires at dusk | The warring armies marched across the plain | The warring kingdoms built a great wall | *The warring neighbours shouted over the fence every morning
exam: What does «warring» mean in «warring kingdoms»? | Shining brightly in the sun | Hungry for food | Tall and thin | *Groups fighting each other
